entire draft sequence at once, producing logits for each position conditioned on the prefix and the previously proposed tokens.\n\nThe key trick is that verification can preserve the target model\u2019s exact output distribution. For each draft token, the target compares its probability for that token with the draft\u2019s probability. If the target is at least as confident, the token is accepted. If not, the token is rejected and a new token is sampled from a corrected distribution that favors the target\u2019s probabilities while accounting for the draft\u2019s proposal. This rejection-sampling step ensures the final sequence is distributed exactly as if the target model had generated every token by itself, assuming the same random seed and numerical behavior.\n\nIn practice, speedup depends on how many draft tokens are accepted before the first rejection, the relative cost of the draft and target models, and the efficiency of the target\u2019s parallel verification. A good draft model that closely matches the target\u2019s preferences can accept multiple tokens per target pass, turning several sequential target steps into one. The speedup is limited by the draft model\u2019s overhead, memory and latency costs, and the fact that verification still requires a full target forward pass.\n\nThe two most common reasons the speedup is smaller than hoped are that the draft model is too weak or too different from the target, causing frequent rejections, and that the draft model or verification overhead is large enough to erase the savings from accepting multiple tokens.\"\n\nCount words. Let's count manually", "top2": [[[1596, -0.005657613277435303], [1421, -5.928379535675049]], [[1144, -8.583106136939023e-06], [513, -11.962079048156738]], [[4087, -0.23524098098278046], [310, -1.6075230836868286]], [[1156, -0.0012465136824175715], [303, -6.875136375427246]], [[579, -0.20923392474651337], [25, -2.0295541286468506]], [[1622, -0.0006111343391239643], [3296, -8.16135311126709